---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import AllegroLocality, { type LocalityData } from '~/islands/AllegroLocality';
import si from '~/data/structures/si-diamond.json';
import snapshot from '~/data/benchmarks/matbench-discovery.json';
import type { Structure } from '~/lib/structure/types';
import { clusterAround, hopsFromCentre, reachByDepth } from '~/lib/models/receptive';

const RC = 5;
const MAX_T = 6;
const big = clusterAround(si as unknown as Structure, MAX_T * RC + 1);
const drawn = big.filter((p) => globalThis.Math.hypot(p[0], p[1], p[2]) < 10);
const data: LocalityData = { rc: RC, mpReach: reachByDepth(hopsFromCentre(big, RC, MAX_T), MAX_T) };
const checkpoints = snapshot.models.filter((m) => m.family_dir === 'allegro');

const assumptions = [
  { term: 'Structure', value: 'Diamond silicon (ASE, a = 5.431 Å) around one atom; cutoff 5 Å.' },
  { term: 'Counting', value: 'Atoms that can affect one atomic energy: for Allegro, those inside the cutoff of that atom; for message passing, those within T hops of the cutoff graph (exact counts on a cluster with no boundary effect).' },
  { term: 'Not shown', value: 'Learned weights. Which neighbours matter, and how much, is decided by training.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Allegro"
  description="Allegro, the strictly local equivariant interatomic potential: features on atom pairs, tensor products with the neighbourhood of one atom, no message passing — and the linear scaling to hundred-million-atom simulations that follows."
>
  <article class="page-grid model-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/models/">Models</a> · Model family</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Allegro</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Equivariant like NequIP, but without message passing. Allegro keeps its features on pairs of atoms and refines them
        with directions from one atom’s neighbourhood only, so however deep it is, each atomic energy depends on a single
        cutoff sphere <Cite id="musaelian2023allegro" locator="Abstract; Sec. II.8" />.
      </p>
      <dl class="glance">
        <div><dt>Symmetry</dt><dd>E(3)-equivariant pair features, invariant energy</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Locality</dt><dd>Strictly local: one cutoff at any depth</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Energy</dt><dd>Sum of pair energies E<sub>ij</sub> (E<sub>ij</sub> ≠ E<sub>ji</sub>)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scaling</dt><dd>Linear in atoms; 100,640,512-atom MD reported</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Introduced</dt><dd>Nature Communications 2023</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Code</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/mir-group/allegro">mir-group/allegro</a></dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="rule-note"><Badge label="schematic" /> This page describes the <strong>architecture</strong>; checkpoints are listed below.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="pairs-h">
      <h2 id="pairs-h" class="h-section">Features on pairs, not atoms</h2>
      <p>
        Every ordered pair <i>ij</i> inside the cutoff carries an invariant feature <b>x</b><sub><i>ij</i></sub> and an
        equivariant feature <b>V</b><sub><i>ij</i></sub>. A layer updates the pair from the spherical harmonics of every
        neighbour <i>k</i> of atom <i>i</i>, weighted by learned functions of the earlier pair scalars; by bilinearity the
        sum over <i>k</i> collapses into one embedded environment and a single tensor product
        <Cite id="musaelian2023allegro" locator="Eqs. 16–19" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\mathbf V^{ij,L} = \mathbf V^{ij,L-1} \otimes \Big( \sum_{k\in\mathcal N(i)} w^{ik,L}\big(\mathbf x^{ik,L-1}\big)\, \vec Y\big(\hat{\mathbf r}_{ik}\big) \Big), \qquad E = \sum_i \sigma_{Z_i} \sum_{j\in\mathcal N(i)} E_{ij} + \mu_{Z_i}`} />
      <p>
        Nothing in (1) reaches beyond atom <i>i</i>’s neighbourhood: no feature is ever sent from one atom to another and
        then on again. That is the whole difference from message passing — and it is what makes the computation for each
        atom independent of the rest of the system.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Strict locality: one pair, one neighbourhood, any depth" labels={['live-calculation', 'schematic']} number={1} id="fig-local" framed={false}>
        <AllegroLocality client:visible positions={drawn} data={data} maxT={MAX_T} />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: diamond silicon. The blue bond is one pair <i>ij</i>; the teal lines are the directions to atom <i>i</i>’s
          other neighbours that update it, all inside one cutoff. Right: how many atoms can affect one atomic energy as
          layers are added — flat for Allegro, growing for message passing.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={assumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Architecture from <Cite id="musaelian2023allegro" locator="Sec. II" />; counts computed at build time from the Atlas silicon structure.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="scale-h">
      <h2 id="scale-h" class="h-section">Why strict locality scales</h2>
      <p>
        Because each atom’s energy depends only on its own cutoff sphere, a simulation box can be split into regions that
        exchange only a thin shell of boundary atoms, exactly as for a classical potential. The paper reports linear
        scaling with the number of atoms and runs a 100,640,512-atom silver system on 128 GPUs through LAMMPS
        <Cite id="musaelian2023allegro" locator="Sec. II.8; Table 4" />.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="caveat" title="The price of locality">
        A strictly local model cannot see anything beyond its cutoff, not even indirectly through intermediate atoms.
        Longer cutoffs help (the paper uses up to 10 Å for molecules), but electrostatics and charge transfer over longer
        distances need an explicit long-range term (<a href="/physics-lab/beyond-the-cutoff/">P1</a>,
        <a href="/physics-lab/ewald/">P6</a>).
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="ck-h">
      <h2 id="ck-h" class="h-section">Checkpoints on Matbench Discovery</h2>
      <p>Universal Allegro checkpoints in the Matbench Discovery snapshot of {snapshot.source.commit_date}; their results are on <a href="/benchmarks/">Benchmarks</a>.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="ck">
          <thead><tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Training data</th><th>Parameters</th><th>Weights licence</th><th>Added</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {checkpoints.map((m) => (
              <tr><td>{m.name}</td><td>{m.training_sets.join(', ')}</td><td>{m.params ? `${(m.params / 1e6).toFixed(1)} M` : '—'}</td><td>{m.licence_checkpoint ?? '—'}</td><td>{m.added}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Related families">
        <a href="/models/nequip/">NequIP</a> shares Allegro’s equivariant features but passes messages between atoms
        <Cite id="batzner2022nequip" />; <a href="/models/mace/">MACE</a> keeps message passing but needs only two layers
        <Cite id="batatia2022mace" />.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .model-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 4.6rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .glance {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 13rem), 1fr));
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .glance > div {
    padding: 0.65rem 0.8rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--atlas-rule);
    border-radius: 0.55rem;
    background: var(--atlas-surface);
  }
  .glance dt {
    font: 600 0.7rem var(--font-sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .glance dd {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
    font: 0.9rem/1.4 var(--font-sans);
  }
  .glance a,
  .block a {
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .rule-note {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    align-items: center;
    font: 0.85rem var(--font-sans);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .ck {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font: 0.86rem/1.45 var(--font-sans);
    min-width: 36rem;
    width: 100%;
  }
  .ck th,
  .ck td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--atlas-rule);
  }
  .ck th {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--atlas-ink-faint);
  }
  .ck td:first-child {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
